    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> It's the Weekend.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            Reading: Find familiar words in a text.<br>
            Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support.<br>
            Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            Reading: Can find and point to familiar words in simple texts (e.g., "Monday," "Tuesday").<br>
            Can identify and follow one-step written directions with visual support (e.g., following the instruction, "Point to the word 'Monday'" in the sentence "Today is Monday," supported by a calendar picture).<br>
            Can identify the sounds of the letters of the alphabet in familiar vocabulary related to days of the week and daily routines when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound /w/ in "Wednesday" supported with a picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 52%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</b></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays large flashcards of the days of the week (Monday-Sunday) and a large picture of a "weekend" activity (e.g., playing in the park). The teacher points to each flashcard and asks, "What day is today?" or "What day is this?" while eliciting simple responses or choral repetition of the day. The teacher uses TPR by showing actions for "relaxing" or "busy" days. Students participate by pointing to the correct flashcard as the teacher names the day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading each day of the week aloud, pointing to the written word on the flashcard. For example, the teacher points to "Monday" and says, "This is Monday. Can you read 'Monday'?" The teacher then points to the word "weekend" and emphasizes the /w/ sound, saying, "Weekend. W-w-weekend." The teacher also points to "day" and emphasizes the /d/ sound, saying, "Day. D-d-day." The teacher explicitly demonstrates finding and pointing to a familiar word in a short sentence written on the board, like "Today is Monday," by circling "Monday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the "Monday" flashcard) "Is this 'Sunday' or 'Monday'?" (Students point to "Monday" or say "Monday.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a written word "day") "Can you see the word 'day'?" (Students nod or point to the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "weekend" on a flashcard) "Does 'weekend' start with /w/ or /m/?" (Students try to make the /w/ sound or say "wuh").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple picture calendar for "All Week Long!" showing each day of the week with a corresponding simple picture of an activity (e.g., "Monday: School," "Saturday: Play," "Sunday: Relax"). Below each day, the name of the day is written clearly. There is also a small written text at the bottom: "Today is Monday. It is a busy day. Saturday and Sunday are the weekend. It is relaxing."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large illustrated weekly calendar/schedule chart, simple picture storybook or large poster with short sentences related to the days of the week and weekend activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using time-related vocabulary: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them. Reading: Find familiar words in a text. Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the sentences aloud slowly, pointing to each word. Then, the teacher says, "Point to the word 'Monday' on the calendar." Students point.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Find the word 'weekend' in the text." Students are encouraged to point to it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Look at the calendar. Point to the day before 'Wednesday'." (Students point to "Tuesday").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is 'Saturday' relaxing or busy?" (Students point to the word "relaxing" in the short text or to the picture for Saturday).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading familiar words (days of the week, weekend, day, week). Grammar: Noun + verb + noun (e.g., "Today is Monday."), Present simple (e.g., "It is the weekend."). Phonemic awareness: identifying /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word-Picture Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with two columns. Column A has pictures representing each day (e.g., a school building for Monday, a sun for Sunday). Column B has the written names of the days of the week and "weekend" in a mixed order. Students draw lines to match the picture to the correct written word. The teacher circulates, checking and prompting, "Can you find 'Friday'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Read and Point (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays a large poster with simple sentences like: "1. Today is Tuesday. 2. It is the weekend! 3. What day is today?" The teacher calls out a sentence number and a target word. For example, "Number 1, point to 'Tuesday'." Students read the sentence silently and point to the target word on their individual copies of the sentences or on the large poster. The teacher then asks, "What sound do you hear in 'day'?" to reinforce /d/ and "What sound in 'weekend'?" for /w/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follow the Written Direction (Pair Work/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a simple calendar worksheet showing the days of the week. Each worksheet has one-step written directions at the bottom with visual support, such as: "Circle 'Sunday'." (picture of Sunday), "Draw a star on 'Friday'." (picture of Friday). Partner A reads a direction to Partner B, who then follows it. Then they switch roles. The teacher encourages them to use polite expressions: "Please circle 'Monday'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: My Weekend Schedule</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with two boxes labeled "Saturday" and "Sunday." Each box has a simple sentence starter, for example, "On Saturday, I..." and "On Sunday, I...". Below these, there are small picture options (e.g., a child sleeping, playing, reading a book, watching TV) with their corresponding simple action words (sleep, play, read, watch). Students read the sentence starters and then read the action words/look at the pictures. Their task is to circle the picture/word that represents what they do on Saturday and Sunday, completing their "weekend schedule." The teacher can also add a simple instruction like, "Circle the word 'relaxing' if your weekend is relaxing." or "Underline 'busy' if your day is busy." to reinforce adjective vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension of simple sentences and one-step directions, identification of familiar words, and making personal connections to the "It's the Weekend" theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage and a short individual "Read & Do" exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an individual worksheet with 3-4 simple sentences/instructions. Each item combines reading familiar words and following a one-step direction with visual support. Examples: "1. Point to 'Monday'." (Next to the word Monday, a picture of a school). "2. Circle the word 'weekend'." (Next to a picture of children playing). "3. Draw a sun on 'Sunday'." (Next to the word Sunday). The teacher also assesses phonemic awareness by asking students to identify the initial sound of 'weekend' and 'day' when pointing to the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in finding and pointing to familiar words (e.g., "Monday," "weekend").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow one-step written directions with visual support (e.g., circling, drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of the /w/ sound in "weekend" and /d/ sound in "day."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "smiley face" card if they feel they can find the words, or a "neutral face" if they need more practice. They also complete two simple prompts on an exit slip:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can find the word: ____________ (e.g., "Monday")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can follow the instruction: ____________ (e.g., "Circle Sunday")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What words did you read and point to today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which day word was easy for you to find?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., visual support, TPR, pair work for reading).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in finding words or following directions? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for the next reading activity? (e.g., more direct modeling, smaller group support, simpler texts).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students identify the /w/ and /d/ sounds?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and identifying days of the week. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively speak and interact using these same vocabulary words and simple sentences (e.g., "What day is today?", "Today is Monday.") to describe their weekly routines and weekend plans.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>